b.com/users/iddocohen/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/iddocohen","id":20091479,"login":"iddocohen","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIwMDkxNDc5","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/iddocohen","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/commits/bee3fed7e9a551d020aaa35a40a636204d50dc5c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:11:36Z","email":"iddocohen@gmail.com","name":"iddocohen"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T21:11:36Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: add Run parallel branches and correct rebase and pool pages (#10838)\n\n* docs: add Run parallel branches and correct rebase and pool pages\n\nAdd a how-to for working on several branches at once and correct the\npages it relies on: rebase order and what blocks it, merge re-checks,\nconflict types and resolution, branch statuses, branch-local data,\nProposed Change checks, upgrade rebase status, webhook branch scope,\nand Resource Manager allocation, identifiers and concurrency.\n\n* docs: add diagrams to branch, conflict and resource pool pages\n\nMermaid diagrams for the parallel-branch workflow, the rebase gate order,\nhow each conflict type clears, branch awareness settings, write blocking\nduring a merge, per-pool request ordering and identifier reuse.\n\n* docs: replace the conflict image with a worked git graph example\n\nThe resolve conflicts page now walks one attribute through a conflict,\na merge resolution and a rebase resolution, each with a Mermaid git\ngraph. The old image showed a rebase resolving in favor of the default\nbranch, which a rebase refuses. The unused SVG is removed.\n\n* docs: replace the rebase image with before and after git graphs\n\nThe rebase page now shows a branch before and after a rebase that\nbrings in a change merged into the default branch, using the same\ngit graph style as the resolve conflicts example. The old image showed\na default branch that never changed. The unused SVG is removed.\n\n* docs: rename Run parallel branches to Parallel branches\n\n\"Run\" promises a set of steps, and the page is mostly explanation: what\nhappens to your branch while other people work, and what Infrahub does\nnot tell you. \"Parallel branches\" is the phrase the team already uses,\nand the Resource Manager tutorial already says \"when multiple teams work\nin parallel branches\".\n\nUpdates the eight links to the page that used the old title. The sidebar\ntakes its label from the page title, so it needs no change. The file name\nand the URL are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: move duplicated rebase and merge content to the pages that own it\n\nThe parallel branches page repeated two pages it links to. The BranchRebase\nexample was identical on both pages, character for character, and the merge\nsection was a shortened copy of the merge page. When the same thing is written\ntwice, one copy gets corrected later and the other does not.\n\nThe table of what blocks a rebase moves the other way. It was on the parallel\nbranches page while the rebase page carried a bullet list with no fixes in it,\nso someone whose rebase had just been refused found the weaker version on the\npage named after what they were doing. The table moves across unchanged; its\nwording is addressed separately.\n\nAlso adds two things the page needed:\n\n- What happens to pool values when a branch is deleted. The page covers what\n  another branch''s creation, rebase and merge do to yours, but not its\n  deletion, which is where the question of whether an address comes back\n  arises. Sourced from branch-agnostic-data.mdx and delete.mdx.\n- A link to the diff recalculation a merge queues on every other open branch,\n  which is a direct consequence of running branches in parallel and was\n  documented only on the performance tuning page.\n\nThe four links that described the page as being about rebasing and merging are\nupdated to describe what it covers now. The page goes from 153 lines to 109.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct ten claims about product behaviour\n\nEach change below gives the reason and the code it was checked against, so\nthis commit can be reviewed from the message. Two are corrections of errors;\neight are imprecisions. Nothing here is a wording change — those come next, in\ntheir own commit.\n\n1. objects/convert-object-kind.mdx — put the data-loss warning back\n\n   This PR removed \"May result in data loss on non-conversion branches\"\n   because nothing stated it in words. It is true. Converting deletes the\n   source node (core/convert_object_type/object_conversion.py:163), and for an\n   agnostic schema Node.delete closes the node edge globally\n   (core/node/__init__.py:1269-1286), so the object goes from every branch. The\n   replacement is created on the default branch only, with that branch''s values\n   (object_conversion.py:188-193). Any value another branch had set on a\n   branch-aware attribute of the original is then unreachable. The code says so\n   itself at object_conversion.py:177-178: \"we need to put other branches in\n   NEED_REBASE state as aware attributes do not exist in other branches after\n   conversion\". No test covers it, so this is read from the code rather than\n   observed — worth confirming on an instance.\n\n2. schema/branch-awareness.mdx — branch-agnostic data does appear in a diff\n\n   The new diagram said an agnostic change is \"not in the diff, merge or\n   rebase\". The rebase half is right: agnostic edges live on the global branch,\n   so RebaseBranchQuery never touches them. The diff half is wrong.\n   core/query/diff.py:388 filters on [$branch_aware, $branch_agnostic], :528\n   and :541 carve out time-filter exemptions for agnostic data, and\n   core/diff/query_parser.py:575-578 forces DiffAction.UPDATED on agnostic\n   nodes. The summary table above the diagram made the same claim and predates\n   this PR; both are corrected together, since leaving one would contradict the\n   other.\n\n3. branches/rebase.mdx — the page no longer contradicts itself\n\n   Line 8 said a rebase moves branched_from \"to the time the rebase starts\".\n   Step 4 says it moves to the end time of the diff from step 1, which is what\n   core/branch/tasks.py:238 does (rebase_at = enriched_diff_metadata.to_time).\n   The opening now says what a rebase is for and leaves the exact moment to\n   step 4, where it is already stated precisely, rather than giving two\n   different answers on one page.\n\n4. branches/rebase.mdx — the branch details button is not silent\n\n   It shows no outcome, which was the point being made, but it does toast\n   \"Branch rebase requested!\" (entities/branches/ui/branch-rebase-button.tsx:44).\n   The outcome never arrives because the mutation returns before the rebase\n   runs, so the error handler at :49-54 cannot fire. Reworded so a reader who\n   sees the toast does not think the page is broken.\n\n5. proposed-changes/resolve-conflict.mdx — either branch, not just the source\n\n   Data Integrity re-runs when the refreshed diff carries new data\n   (core/diff/data_check_synchronizer.py:62-67), and the diff is recalculated\n   when anything changed in the time range on either branch\n   (core/diff/coordinator.py:673-684). A change on the default branch re-runs it\n   too.\n\n6. resource-manager/overview.mdx — concurrent requests, with the identifier case\n\n   \"Two requests at the same time get different values\" is true only when\n   neither carries an identifier. With the same identifier they serialise on the\n   pool lock and the second gets the first''s reservation\n   (core/node/resource_manager/ip_address_pool.py:44-62). As written it\n   contradicted the identifier rules four paragraphs below.\n\n7. resource-manager/overview.mdx — the pointer named the wrong rules\n\n   \"Described in the next two rules\" skipped the case it most needed: a number\n   pool chosen in a web form is keyed on the object (core/node/__init__.py:490),\n   which is the third rule.\n\n8. branches/overview.mdx — the OPEN row''s \"Cleared by\" cell said nothing\n\n   \"The events that set another status\" tells a reader nothing the other five\n   rows do not. Replaced with an em dash.\n\n9. branches/overview.mdx — NEED_UPGRADE_REBASE has a second setter\n\n   Besides infrahub upgrade (cli/upgrade.py:199, cli/db.py:958-970), it is also\n   set while a branch migration runs (core/branch/tasks.py:144-145).\n\n10. deploy-manage/maintain-upgrade/upgrade/overview.mdx — a claim with no source\n\n    \"Rewrites data that open branches read from the default branch\" is a\n    plausible gloss, but the class contract (core/migrations/shared.py:318-327)\n    only distinguishes execute from execute_against_branch, which \"assumes that\n    the branches have been rebased\". The entry now says what the code says.\n\n11. webhooks/overview.mdx — two scopes deliver the merge event\n\n    The advice to use All Branches is right. Other Branches also matches: its\n    label is \"!main\" and the merged event carries the global branch label\n    \"-global-\" (webhook/models.py:61-65, core/merge/post_merge.py:129-133,\n    core/constants/__init__.py:30). The page implied All Branches was the only\n    scope that worked.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors and\nVale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the eight changed files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: wording pass over the new and rewritten text\n\nNo claim about product behaviour changes here. Grouped so each kind can be\nskimmed separately.\n\nA. \"A branch reads the default branch\" — 9 places\n   branches/create.mdx, parallel-branches.mdx x2, overview.mdx x2, rebase.mdx\n   x3, branch-agnostic-data.mdx\n\n   A branch does not read anything: you query it, and Infrahub returns data.\n   The phrase was not in our docs before this PR, and it is the central\n   sentence of the parallel branches page. Where the idea is introduced, the\n   sentence now names both halves instead of joining them with \"plus\", because\n   \"plus the branch''s own changes\" left it unclear what was being combined:\n\n     When you query a branch, Infrahub combines two things: the default\n     branch''s data as of the branch''s `branched_from` timestamp, and every\n     change made on the branch since.\n\n   \"As it was at\" became \"as of\" throughout, which is shorter and reads better\n   repeated.\n\nB. Five verbs that do not mean what they say\n\n   - conflicts \"surface\" (x2) -> Infrahub reports them\n   - a failed merge \"awaits recovery\" -> is waiting to be recovered\n   - \"route a refusal\" -> handle a refusal\n   - a conflict \"goes away\" -> is cleared\n   - a rebase \"replays it on a base\" -> moves its starting point. That is Git''s\n     model; an Infrahub rebase moves a timestamp and replays nothing.\n\nC. \"Behind the default branch\" — 6 places\n\n   Git''s word, never explained, and it appeared in the opening sentence of the\n   parallel branches page before the mechanism was described. Replacing it with\n   \"out of date\" would have been vaguer still, because neither says out of date\n   against what. Each place now states the condition, and the branch statuses\n   section is rewritten around the question its readers actually arrive with —\n   something is blocked, which situation am I in:\n\n     Infrahub blocks some actions on a branch while a merge is running, after a\n     merge has failed, or when the branch needs a rebase. It marks each of\n     these with a branch status:\n\n     [the table]\n\n     `OPEN` does not mean a branch is up to date with the default branch.\n     Rebase the branch to include changes merged there since.\n\n   The old intro said it \"blocks some operations\", which is what the table''s\n   Blocks column is for, and the old closing line said no status means \"behind\n   the default branch\", which left the reader to work out what to do.\n\nD. Marketing wording carried into a rewritten paragraph — branches/overview.mdx\n\n   Two sentences survived a paragraph this PR otherwise rewrote: feature\n   branches \"strategically relax some of these constraints to optimize for\n   development speed and flexibility\", and \"this balanced approach maintains\n   data integrity where it matters most while enabling rapid iteration\".\n   Both would be true of any product.\n\n   They are deleted with nothing in their place. A replacement would have had\n   to say which of the three trade-offs listed above them is lighter on a\n   feature branch, and that is not established anywhere in the code — inventing\n   a specific claim to replace a vague one is worse, because it reads as though\n   someone checked. If there is a real difference there, it is worth stating\n   and Iddo is better placed to know.\n\nE. One term spelled two ways — schema/branch-awareness.mdx\n\n   \"branch agnostic\" and \"branch local\" in the bullet list; \"branch-agnostic\"\n   and \"branch-local\" everywhere else on the same page. Both bullets are\n   hyphenated now. Only the second is touched by this PR, but fixing one and\n   leaving its neighbour would read as a typo.\n\nF. Four link descriptions, which were mine from the previous commit\n\n   \"What a merge elsewhere does to your branch, and what Infrahub does not tell\n   you\" describes a page by what it lacks. The siblings in those Related lists\n   all list what a page covers, in about ten words, so these now match. The\n   Resource Manager one also overlapped its neighbour, \"Branch-agnostic data\",\n   and now says the thing that page alone covers.\n\nG. The object conversion page opened on two paragraphs of marketing\n\n   \"Provides a powerful mechanism\", \"addresses the common infrastructure\n   management challenge\", \"eliminates this friction by providing a seamless\n   Transformation process\". The opening now says what a conversion does and\n   uses two examples the page already carries further down — a Layer 2\n   interface to Layer 3, a read-only repository to read-write. The \"##\n   Introduction\" section is removed: once the opening does that job, and\n   \"Common use cases\" carries the specifics, it had none. That is the one\n   structural change in a wording commit.\n\nH. An unsourced claim — proposed-changes/overview.mdx\n\n   \"Tamper-evident history: ensures the integrity of the audit trail through\n   cryptographic verification.\" Nothing supports it. Replaced with what is\n   true: Infrahub keeps earlier values and relationships rather than\n   overwriting them.\n\nNot changed: the three \"Performance trade-offs\" bullets above the paragraph in\ngroup D are vague in the same way, but two of the three cannot be sourced, so\nrewriting them would mean guessing. Raised separately.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors over\n12 files, and Vale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings over 10.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: revert a wrong webhook claim and add two clarifications Iddo asked for\n\nRevert, in webhooks/overview.mdx: an earlier commit of mine added \"A webhook\nset to Other Branches also receives it, but All Branches says what you mean.\"\nI worked that out by reading code — Other Branches compiles to a \"!main\" label\nand the merged event carries \"-global-\", so it looked like a match. Iddo tested\nit on 1.11.4: Other Branches caught one of three merge events, All Branches\ncaught all three. The sentence is gone and the warning reads as he wrote it.\n\nAddition, in resource-manager/overview.mdx: what an IP pool creates. The section\ntalks about \"the object it was allocated to\", which is ambiguous on the IP pages\nbecause allocation produces two objects and the page never said so. Allocating\nthrough a relationship finds the next free address, creates an IP address object\nfor it and links that object to the device\n(core/node/resource_manager/ip_address_pool.py:76-90). Deleting the device\nleaves the address object in place, so the pool still counts it as in use. The\nexception is a relationship that cascades, which includes any relationship whose\nkind is Component, since SchemaBranch.process_relationships fills an unset\non_delete with CASCADE for those (core/schema/schema_branch.py:1693-1711); one\nof our own example schemas relies on it (models/examples/slurpit/slurpit.yml:378-380).\n\nAddition, in branches/overview.mdx: which statuses show a badge. The statuses\ntable lists six, and only three of them appear in the interface, so a reader\ncannot tell which they will actually see. Taken from\nfrontend/app/src/entities/branches/ui/branch-list-item/branch-status-badge.tsx,\nwhich returns null for MERGING and MERGE_FAILED. This covers more statuses than\nIddo''s note mentioned; flagged to him in the PR comment.\n\nNot changed: the review bot''s remaining findings are all in Iddo''s text and are\nhis to judge, including line 160 of branches/overview.mdx, where the sentence\nabout changes not being visible until a rebase sits against line 69 of the same\npage saying branch-agnostic records are visible immediately. We could not find\nwording that fixes it without inventing a term, so it is left alone.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors and\nVale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the three changed files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct five behaviour claims and six review findings\n\nBehaviour claims:\n- Branch-agnostic data is not in a branch diff. The diff reads attribute\n  edges on the branch and only for branch-aware attributes\n  (core/query/diff.py:493-497); agnostic values live on the global branch.\n  An agnostic object appears only through its branch-aware attributes.\n- The identifier exception for concurrent requests applies to IP address and\n  IP prefix pools, on the same branch. Number pools ignore identifiers.\n- Branch badges include Open on the branch details page and Deleting\n  (branch-status-badge.tsx).\n- Statuses also block actions after a branch has merged.\n- Conflicts are also reported on a diff refresh and in Proposed Change checks,\n  not only on rebase and merge.\n\nReview findings:\n- An interrupted merge leaves the branch in MERGE_FAILED until\n  infrahub recover merge runs (core/merge/failure_identifier.py:99).\n- Object conversion marks every branch that is not merged or being deleted\n  (core/branch/enums.py:14).\n- A refused rebase changes no data on the branch, but its refreshed diff stays\n  stored.\n- BranchMerge merges into the default branch, matching BranchRebase.\n- \"labelled\" becomes \"labeled\".\n- HFID is spelled out at first use on the IP prefix page.\n- Branch-agnostic data is named as the exception to changes not being visible\n  until a rebase.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Yvonne Jouffrault <yvonne@opsmill.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4234bb4681d1108580e8a824ceb2d4725e30fe8f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/git/trees/4234bb4681d1108580e8a824ceb2d4725e30fe8f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/git/commits/bee3fed7e9a551d020aaa35a40a636204d50dc5c","verification":{"payload":"tree 4234bb4681d1108580e8a824ceb2d4725e30fe8f\nparent cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16\nauthor iddocohen <iddocohen@gmail.com> 1791321096 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791321096 -0400\n\ndocs: add Run parallel branches and correct rebase and pool pages (#10838)\n\n* docs: add Run parallel branches and correct rebase and pool pages\n\nAdd a how-to for working on several branches at once and correct the\npages it relies on: rebase order and what blocks it, merge re-checks,\nconflict types and resolution, branch statuses, branch-local data,\nProposed Change checks, upgrade rebase status, webhook branch scope,\nand Resource Manager allocation, identifiers and concurrency.\n\n* docs: add diagrams to branch, conflict and resource pool pages\n\nMermaid diagrams for the parallel-branch workflow, the rebase gate order,\nhow each conflict type clears, branch awareness settings, write blocking\nduring a merge, per-pool request ordering and identifier reuse.\n\n* docs: replace the conflict image with a worked git graph example\n\nThe resolve conflicts page now walks one attribute through a conflict,\na merge resolution and a rebase resolution, each with a Mermaid git\ngraph. The old image showed a rebase resolving in favor of the default\nbranch, which a rebase refuses. The unused SVG is removed.\n\n* docs: replace the rebase image with before and after git graphs\n\nThe rebase page now shows a branch before and after a rebase that\nbrings in a change merged into the default branch, using the same\ngit graph style as the resolve conflicts example. The old image showed\na default branch that never changed. The unused SVG is removed.\n\n* docs: rename Run parallel branches to Parallel branches\n\n\"Run\" promises a set of steps, and the page is mostly explanation: what\nhappens to your branch while other people work, and what Infrahub does\nnot tell you. \"Parallel branches\" is the phrase the team already uses,\nand the Resource Manager tutorial already says \"when multiple teams work\nin parallel branches\".\n\nUpdates the eight links to the page that used the old title. The sidebar\ntakes its label from the page title, so it needs no change. The file name\nand the URL are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: move duplicated rebase and merge content to the pages that own it\n\nThe parallel branches page repeated two pages it links to. The BranchRebase\nexample was identical on both pages, character for character, and the merge\nsection was a shortened copy of the merge page. When the same thing is written\ntwice, one copy gets corrected later and the other does not.\n\nThe table of what blocks a rebase moves the other way. It was on the parallel\nbranches page while the rebase page carried a bullet list with no fixes in it,\nso someone whose rebase had just been refused found the weaker version on the\npage named after what they were doing. The table moves across unchanged; its\nwording is addressed separately.\n\nAlso adds two things the page needed:\n\n- What happens to pool values when a branch is deleted. The page covers what\n  another branch''s creation, rebase and merge do to yours, but not its\n  deletion, which is where the question of whether an address comes back\n  arises. Sourced from branch-agnostic-data.mdx and delete.mdx.\n- A link to the diff recalculation a merge queues on every other open branch,\n  which is a direct consequence of running branches in parallel and was\n  documented only on the performance tuning page.\n\nThe four links that described the page as being about rebasing and merging are\nupdated to describe what it covers now. The page goes from 153 lines to 109.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct ten claims about product behaviour\n\nEach change below gives the reason and the code it was checked against, so\nthis commit can be reviewed from the message. Two are corrections of errors;\neight are imprecisions. Nothing here is a wording change — those come next, in\ntheir own commit.\n\n1. objects/convert-object-kind.mdx — put the data-loss warning back\n\n   This PR removed \"May result in data loss on non-conversion branches\"\n   because nothing stated it in words. It is true. Converting deletes the\n   source node (core/convert_object_type/object_conversion.py:163), and for an\n   agnostic schema Node.delete closes the node edge globally\n   (core/node/__init__.py:1269-1286), so the object goes from every branch. The\n   replacement is created on the default branch only, with that branch''s values\n   (object_conversion.py:188-193). Any value another branch had set on a\n   branch-aware attribute of the original is then unreachable. The code says so\n   itself at object_conversion.py:177-178: \"we need to put other branches in\n   NEED_REBASE state as aware attributes do not exist in other branches after\n   conversion\". No test covers it, so this is read from the code rather than\n   observed — worth confirming on an instance.\n\n2. schema/branch-awareness.mdx — branch-agnostic data does appear in a diff\n\n   The new diagram said an agnostic change is \"not in the diff, merge or\n   rebase\". The rebase half is right: agnostic edges live on the global branch,\n   so RebaseBranchQuery never touches them. The diff half is wrong.\n   core/query/diff.py:388 filters on [$branch_aware, $branch_agnostic], :528\n   and :541 carve out time-filter exemptions for agnostic data, and\n   core/diff/query_parser.py:575-578 forces DiffAction.UPDATED on agnostic\n   nodes. The summary table above the diagram made the same claim and predates\n   this PR; both are corrected together, since leaving one would contradict the\n   other.\n\n3. branches/rebase.mdx — the page no longer contradicts itself\n\n   Line 8 said a rebase moves branched_from \"to the time the rebase starts\".\n   Step 4 says it moves to the end time of the diff from step 1, which is what\n   core/branch/tasks.py:238 does (rebase_at = enriched_diff_metadata.to_time).\n   The opening now says what a rebase is for and leaves the exact moment to\n   step 4, where it is already stated precisely, rather than giving two\n   different answers on one page.\n\n4. branches/rebase.mdx — the branch details button is not silent\n\n   It shows no outcome, which was the point being made, but it does toast\n   \"Branch rebase requested!\" (entities/branches/ui/branch-rebase-button.tsx:44).\n   The outcome never arrives because the mutation returns before the rebase\n   runs, so the error handler at :49-54 cannot fire. Reworded so a reader who\n   sees the toast does not think the page is broken.\n\n5. proposed-changes/resolve-conflict.mdx — either branch, not just the source\n\n   Data Integrity re-runs when the refreshed diff carries new data\n   (core/diff/data_check_synchronizer.py:62-67), and the diff is recalculated\n   when anything changed in the time range on either branch\n   (core/diff/coordinator.py:673-684). A change on the default branch re-runs it\n   too.\n\n6. resource-manager/overview.mdx — concurrent requests, with the identifier case\n\n   \"Two requests at the same time get different values\" is true only when\n   neither carries an identifier. With the same identifier they serialise on the\n   pool lock and the second gets the first''s reservation\n   (core/node/resource_manager/ip_address_pool.py:44-62). As written it\n   contradicted the identifier rules four paragraphs below.\n\n7. resource-manager/overview.mdx — the pointer named the wrong rules\n\n   \"Described in the next two rules\" skipped the case it most needed: a number\n   pool chosen in a web form is keyed on the object (core/node/__init__.py:490),\n   which is the third rule.\n\n8. branches/overview.mdx — the OPEN row''s \"Cleared by\" cell said nothing\n\n   \"The events that set another status\" tells a reader nothing the other five\n   rows do not. Replaced with an em dash.\n\n9. branches/overview.mdx — NEED_UPGRADE_REBASE has a second setter\n\n   Besides infrahub upgrade (cli/upgrade.py:199, cli/db.py:958-970), it is also\n   set while a branch migration runs (core/branch/tasks.py:144-145).\n\n10. deploy-manage/maintain-upgrade/upgrade/overview.mdx — a claim with no source\n\n    \"Rewrites data that open branches read from the default branch\" is a\n    plausible gloss, but the class contract (core/migrations/shared.py:318-327)\n    only distinguishes execute from execute_against_branch, which \"assumes that\n    the branches have been rebased\". The entry now says what the code says.\n\n11. webhooks/overview.mdx — two scopes deliver the merge event\n\n    The advice to use All Branches is right. Other Branches also matches: its\n    label is \"!main\" and the merged event carries the global branch label\n    \"-global-\" (webhook/models.py:61-65, core/merge/post_merge.py:129-133,\n    core/constants/__init__.py:30). The page implied All Branches was the only\n    scope that worked.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors and\nVale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the eight changed files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: wording pass over the new and rewritten text\n\nNo claim about product behaviour changes here. Grouped so each kind can be\nskimmed separately.\n\nA. \"A branch reads the default branch\" — 9 places\n   branches/create.mdx, parallel-branches.mdx x2, overview.mdx x2, rebase.mdx\n   x3, branch-agnostic-data.mdx\n\n   A branch does not read anything: you query it, and Infrahub returns data.\n   The phrase was not in our docs before this PR, and it is the central\n   sentence of the parallel branches page. Where the idea is introduced, the\n   sentence now names both halves instead of joining them with \"plus\", because\n   \"plus the branch''s own changes\" left it unclear what was being combined:\n\n     When you query a branch, Infrahub combines two things: the default\n     branch''s data as of the branch''s `branched_from` timestamp, and every\n     change made on the branch since.\n\n   \"As it was at\" became \"as of\" throughout, which is shorter and reads better\n   repeated.\n\nB. Five verbs that do not mean what they say\n\n   - conflicts \"surface\" (x2) -> Infrahub reports them\n   - a failed merge \"awaits recovery\" -> is waiting to be recovered\n   - \"route a refusal\" -> handle a refusal\n   - a conflict \"goes away\" -> is cleared\n   - a rebase \"replays it on a base\" -> moves its starting point. That is Git''s\n     model; an Infrahub rebase moves a timestamp and replays nothing.\n\nC. \"Behind the default branch\" — 6 places\n\n   Git''s word, never explained, and it appeared in the opening sentence of the\n   parallel branches page before the mechanism was described. Replacing it with\n   \"out of date\" would have been vaguer still, because neither says out of date\n   against what. Each place now states the condition, and the branch statuses\n   section is rewritten around the question its readers actually arrive with —\n   something is blocked, which situation am I in:\n\n     Infrahub blocks some actions on a branch while a merge is running, after a\n     merge has failed, or when the branch needs a rebase. It marks each of\n     these with a branch status:\n\n     [the table]\n\n     `OPEN` does not mean a branch is up to date with the default branch.\n     Rebase the branch to include changes merged there since.\n\n   The old intro said it \"blocks some operations\", which is what the table''s\n   Blocks column is for, and the old closing line said no status means \"behind\n   the default branch\", which left the reader to work out what to do.\n\nD. Marketing wording carried into a rewritten paragraph — branches/overview.mdx\n\n   Two sentences survived a paragraph this PR otherwise rewrote: feature\n   branches \"strategically relax some of these constraints to optimize for\n   development speed and flexibility\", and \"this balanced approach maintains\n   data integrity where it matters most while enabling rapid iteration\".\n   Both would be true of any product.\n\n   They are deleted with nothing in their place. A replacement would have had\n   to say which of the three trade-offs listed above them is lighter on a\n   feature branch, and that is not established anywhere in the code — inventing\n   a specific claim to replace a vague one is worse, because it reads as though\n   someone checked. If there is a real difference there, it is worth stating\n   and Iddo is better placed to know.\n\nE. One term spelled two ways — schema/branch-awareness.mdx\n\n   \"branch agnostic\" and \"branch local\" in the bullet list; \"branch-agnostic\"\n   and \"branch-local\" everywhere else on the same page. Both bullets are\n   hyphenated now. Only the second is touched by this PR, but fixing one and\n   leaving its neighbour would read as a typo.\n\nF. Four link descriptions, which were mine from the previous commit\n\n   \"What a merge elsewhere does to your branch, and what Infrahub does not tell\n   you\" describes a page by what it lacks. The siblings in those Related lists\n   all list what a page covers, in about ten words, so these now match. The\n   Resource Manager one also overlapped its neighbour, \"Branch-agnostic data\",\n   and now says the thing that page alone covers.\n\nG. The object conversion page opened on two paragraphs of marketing\n\n   \"Provides a powerful mechanism\", \"addresses the common infrastructure\n   management challenge\", \"eliminates this friction by providing a seamless\n   Transformation process\". The opening now says what a conversion does and\n   uses two examples the page already carries further down — a Layer 2\n   interface to Layer 3, a read-only repository to read-write. The \"##\n   Introduction\" section is removed: once the opening does that job, and\n   \"Common use cases\" carries the specifics, it had none. That is the one\n   structural change in a wording commit.\n\nH. An unsourced claim — proposed-changes/overview.mdx\n\n   \"Tamper-evident history: ensures the integrity of the audit trail through\n   cryptographic verification.\" Nothing supports it. Replaced with what is\n   true: Infrahub keeps earlier values and relationships rather than\n   overwriting them.\n\nNot changed: the three \"Performance trade-offs\" bullets above the paragraph in\ngroup D are vague in the same way, but two of the three cannot be sourced, so\nrewriting them would mean guessing. Raised separately.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors over\n12 files, and Vale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings over 10.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: revert a wrong webhook claim and add two clarifications Iddo asked for\n\nRevert, in webhooks/overview.mdx: an earlier commit of mine added \"A webhook\nset to Other Branches also receives it, but All Branches says what you mean.\"\nI worked that out by reading code — Other Branches compiles to a \"!main\" label\nand the merged event carries \"-global-\", so it looked like a match. Iddo tested\nit on 1.11.4: Other Branches caught one of three merge events, All Branches\ncaught all three. The sentence is gone and the warning reads as he wrote it.\n\nAddition, in resource-manager/overview.mdx: what an IP pool creates. The section\ntalks about \"the object it was allocated to\", which is ambiguous on the IP pages\nbecause allocation produces two objects and the page never said so. Allocating\nthrough a relationship finds the next free address, creates an IP address object\nfor it and links that object to the device\n(core/node/resource_manager/ip_address_pool.py:76-90). Deleting the device\nleaves the address object in place, so the pool still counts it as in use. The\nexception is a relationship that cascades, which includes any relationship whose\nkind is Component, since SchemaBranch.process_relationships fills an unset\non_delete with CASCADE for those (core/schema/schema_branch.py:1693-1711); one\nof our own example schemas relies on it (models/examples/slurpit/slurpit.yml:378-380).\n\nAddition, in branches/overview.mdx: which statuses show a badge. The statuses\ntable lists six, and only three of them appear in the interface, so a reader\ncannot tell which they will actually see. Taken from\nfrontend/app/src/entities/branches/ui/branch-list-item/branch-status-badge.tsx,\nwhich returns null for MERGING and MERGE_FAILED. This covers more statuses than\nIddo''s note mentioned; flagged to him in the PR comment.\n\nNot changed: the review bot''s remaining findings are all in Iddo''s text and are\nhis to judge, including line 160 of branches/overview.mdx, where the sentence\nabout changes not being visible until a rebase sits against line 69 of the same\npage saying branch-agnostic records are visible immediately. We could not find\nwording that fixes it without inventing a term, so it is left alone.\n\nThe docs build passes with no broken links, markdownlint reports 0 errors and\nVale reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the three changed files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs: correct five behaviour claims and six review findings\n\nBehaviour claims:\n- Branch-agnostic data is not in a branch diff. The diff reads attribute\n  edges on the branch and only for branch-aware attributes\n  (core/query/diff.py:493-497); agnostic values live on the global branch.\n  An agnostic object appears only through its branch-aware attributes.\n- The identifier exception for concurrent requests applies to IP address and\n  IP prefix pools, on the same branch. Number pools ignore identifiers.\n- Branch badges include Open on the branch details page and Deleting\n  (branch-status-badge.tsx).\n- Statuses also block actions after a branch has merged.\n- Conflicts are also reported on a diff refresh and in Proposed Change checks,\n  not only on rebase and merge.\n\nReview findings:\n- An interrupted merge leaves the branch in MERGE_FAILED until\n  infrahub recover merge runs (core/merge/failure_identifier.py:99).\n- Object conversion marks every branch that is not merged or being deleted\n  (core/branch/enums.py:14).\n- A refused rebase changes no data on the branch, but its refreshed diff stays\n  stored.\n- BranchMerge merges into the default branch, matching BranchRebase.\n- \"labelled\" becomes \"labeled\".\n- HFID is spelled out at first use on the IP prefix page.\n- Branch-agnostic data is named as the exception to changes not being visible\n  until a rebase.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Yvonne Jouffrault <yvonne@opsmill.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxWQICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1zgQAEaPycDe5+luYOepaKE7s4CO\nQ+HQB+CNW7DO5era1FMT7OT1Y7IQv5OsBZzE9wp4EGiRHNk2AgQUdEW/IU3bY92r\n+/GNLChXeUrwJsWpbGZE2c2/pB3XMRSl3LUNikEpVFMs/gobjr6csBpygSFU4XY4\nYIxqBdzVcdzVZGQhg/rXBy74k/QgVTFRPgur08hMddryv+ylIbm72V3jLvGOQWPO\nHMC3rzd8hFopOpVLhkHjfdb3tYsYib0rTDUiQymcj6fmiwVyqwnrKBXHEaAcKDXj\nDdF4MYBsyUPQZmkzUBLbXfmscChKoa8nd1qDlPxEObVEF6HgbDMHxEEwh/NcYKWH\nH8ovKQdIUFFWld7SXdg0IOb04SJ+zn1r9J/ewET/7fjWhEonRf5BMYMdhzE+241q\npbKZi73N1mqjWpRWegjabMRjvZAXGMZITNXEbNJTS5YwJAU+hmB6LQDpvS7aNRxQ\nHCDVZrHWizHK135T3/YJ9pTiFCJe4PVGLpPtKMD9eSG9kcjiqlhAKkdwcAmLnT+P\nfXrvXlNzd4oZ9lvJx2EKoBUzJPO/VkEdNBTz54lWFLxElAjSJ7ohc3mOBATj11ML\nxiCEkaWdHqERjW6gshbsTtHPGO+RPhZ8vPn02qkbJZ2pAKfGLLi2wP1F13svU6Iu\n2IaiSOZjDijbFxgyNs63\n=0i6/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T21:11:37Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/opsmill/infrahub/commit/bee3fed7e9a551d020aaa35a40a636204d50dc5c","node_id":"C_kwDOH4TYMtoAKGJlZTNmZWQ3ZTlhNTUxZDAyMGFhYTM1YTQwYTYzNjIwNGQ1MGRjNWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/opsmill/infrahub/commit/cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16","sha":"cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/commits/cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16"}],"sha":"bee3fed7e9a551d020aaa35a40a636204d50dc5c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/commits/bee3fed7e9a551d020aaa35a40a636204d50dc5c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/15846100?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/saltas888","id":15846100,"login":"saltas888","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1ODQ2MTAw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/saltas888","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/commits/cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T15:41:41Z","email":"d.saltaferis@gmail.com","name":"Saltaferis Dimitrios"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T15:41:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(labels): add category/derived-values to the triage labels (#10830)\n\nBugs about computed attributes, display labels and HFIDs are spread\nacross five categories, so there is no single view of them. The new\ncategory goes on top of the primary category, so the labeler can now\nadd three labels and the sweep 45.\n\nBoth locks are recompiled with gh-aw v0.81.6. The recompile resets the\naction pins Dependabot bumped in the lock files (checkout v7.0.1,\nsetup-node v7.0.0, cache v6.1.0), so those are restored.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2c88303b21d716f1244b88f2ee4bdb3a0525a8b4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/git/trees/2c88303b21d716f1244b88f2ee4bdb3a0525a8b4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/opsmill/infrahub/git/commits/cd99951d3bdcd4778a33ff4dfd6ce9410f74fe16","verification":{"payload":"tree 2c88303b21d716f1244b88f2ee4bdb3a0525a8b4\nparent d6a0fbcd59e892dda9efc415733bbea61a0e5edc\nauthor Saltaferis Dimitrios <d.saltaferis@gmail.com> 1791301301 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791301301 +0200\n\nchore(labels): add category/derived-values to the triage labels (#10830)\n\nBugs about computed attributes, display labels and HFIDs are spread\nacross five categories, so there is no single view of them. The new\ncategory goes on top of the primary category, so the labeler can now\nadd three labels and the sweep 45.\n\nBoth locks are recompiled with gh-aw v0.81.6. The recompile resets the\naction pins Dependabot bumped in the lock files (checkout v7.0.1,\nsetup-node v7.0.0, cache v6.1.0), so those are restored.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","r